# Question 07 :
How do the relative, absolute, and fixed positioning properties differ in
CSS?

# Answer 07 :
In CSS, the position property is used to control how an element is placed on a webpage. Three common positioning types are relative, absolute, and fixed.

1.  Relative Positioning

    When an element has position: relative, it is positioned relative to its normal position in the document. The element can be moved using properties like top, left, right, and bottom.

    .box {
    position: relative;
    top: 10px;
    left: 20px;
    } 2. Absolute Positioning

    When an element has position: absolute, it is positioned relative to its nearest positioned ancestor (an ancestor with position other than static). If no such ancestor exists, it is positioned relative to the entire webpage.

    .box {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50px;
    left: 100px;
    } 3. Fixed Positioning

    When an element has position: fixed, it is positioned relative to the browser window (viewport). It stays in the same position even when the page is scrolled.

    .box {
    position: fixed;
    bottom: 10px;
    right: 10px;
    }


